{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-208021",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{7}\\times \\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($2$) and the second fraction's denominator ($4$) have a common \nfactor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{4}\\times \\frac{5}{7}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the first fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2÷2}{4÷2}\\times \\frac{5}{7} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{5}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{5}{7}=\\frac{1\\times 5}{2\\times 7}=\\frac{5}{14}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
